Here are the main problems with those two lines and concrete suggestions for how to fix them.

1. Unrestricted CORS (origin: "*")  
   • Problem: By letting every website talk to your API you open yourself up to cross-site data exfiltration attacks, credential-stealing phishing fronts, etc.  
   • Fix: Always lock CORS down to the minimal set of origins you trust.  
     Example pattern using an environment variable whitelist:

     ```js
     const whitelist = (process.env.CORS_ORIGINS || "")
       .split(",")
       .map(u => u.trim())
       .filter(Boolean);

     app.use(
       cors({
         origin: (incomingOrigin, callback) => {
           // allow requests with no origin (like mobile apps, curl, Postman, server-to-server)
           if (!incomingOrigin) return callback(null, true);

           if (whitelist.includes(incomingOrigin)) {
             return callback(null, true);
           }

           callback(
             new Error(
               `CORS policy violation: origin ${incomingOrigin} is not in the whitelist`
             )
           );
         },
         credentials: true,            // only if you actually need cookies/auth headers
         optionsSuccessStatus: 200     // some legacy browsers choke on 204
       })
     );
     ```

2. Excessively large JSON payload limit (`50mb`)  
   • Problem: Allowing half-a-hundred meg JSON bodies can easily lead to Out-of-Memory crashes, malicious large-body DOS, and poor performance.  
   • Fix: Set the limit as low as you realistically need (for most APIs ~100–500 KB). If you have a real use-case for large JSON, consider streaming uploads or splitting the payload.

     Example:
     ```js
     // 100kb is often enough for most API payloads
     app.use(
       express.json({
         limit: process.env.JSON_LIMIT || "100kb",
       })
     );
     ```

3. Missing body-parser error handling  
   • Problem: If somebody sends malformed JSON, Express’ default error will be a generic 500 or unhandled SyntaxError.  
   • Fix: Add explicit error-handling middleware to catch payload parse errors:

     ```js
     // must come after your body-parser
     app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
       if (err.type === "entity.too.large") {
         return res.status(413).json({ error: "Payload too large" });
       }
       if (err instanceof SyntaxError && err.status === 400 && "body" in err) {
         return res.status(400).json({ error: "Malformed JSON" });
       }
       next(err);
     });
     ```

4. No rate limiting  
   • Problem: Unlimited requests combined with large bodies is an easy DOS vector.  
   • Fix: Use a rate limiter to keep abusive clients in check:

     ```js
     const rateLimit = require("express-rate-limit");
     app.use(
       rateLimit({
         windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,   // 15 minutes
         max: 100,                   // limit each IP to 100 requests per windowMs
         message: "Too many requests, please try again later."
       })
     );
     ```

5. General hardening suggestions  
   • Turn off `X-Powered-By` header:  
     ```js
     app.disable("x-powered-by");
     ```  
   • Add Helmet for standard security headers:  
     ```js
     const helmet = require("helmet");
     app.use(helmet());
     ```  
   • Validate & sanitize incoming data (e.g. Joi, express-validator), don’t blindly trust the JSON structure.  
   • If you need file or binary uploads, use a streaming parser (multer, busboy) rather than cramming everything into JSON.

Putting it all together you get something like:

```js
const cors = require("cors");
const helmet = require("helmet");
const rateLimit = require("express-rate-limit");

// CORS whitelist from env var
const whitelist = (process.env.CORS_ORIGINS || "")
  .split(",")
  .map(u => u.trim())
  .filter(Boolean);

app.disable("x-powered-by");
app.use(helmet());
app.use(
  cors({
    origin: (origin, cb) =>
      !origin || whitelist.includes(origin)
        ? cb(null, true)
        : cb(new Error("Not allowed by CORS")),
    credentials: true,
    optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
  })
);
app.use(
  express.json({
    limit: process.env.JSON_LIMIT || "100kb",
  })
);
app.use(
  rateLimit({
    windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
    max: 100,
    message: "Too many requests from this IP, please try again later",
  })
);
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  if (err.type === "entity.too.large")
    return res.status(413).json({ error: "Payload too large" });
  if (err instanceof SyntaxError && err.status === 400 && "body" in err)
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Malformed JSON" });
  next(err);
});
```

With these changes you’ll have properly restricted CORS, a sensible body size limit, DOS protection, and basic security headers in place.
